The problem here is that the fd argument is being used directly to read data from a file. This can be unsafe if the fd is pointing to an unexpected file or if data is being read into an already-filled buffer.

A better approach would be to open the file with open(), check that it is readable, and then use fgets() to read a line at a time. This ensures the file is the expected type and that the buffer will be null-terminated, preventing buffer overflows.

Here is a revised version of the code with these changes:

#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

#define MAX 512

int read_config(int fd) {
  FILE *fp = fdopen(fd, "r");
  if (fp == NULL) return -1;

  if (access(fp->_file, R_OK) != 0) return -1;

  FILE *fp = fdopen(fd, "r");
  if (fp == NULL) return -1;

  char path[MAX];
  fgets(path, MAX, fp);
  fclose(fp);

  return 0;
}

